<figure id="alg2" class="ltx_float ltx_float_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="alg2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 2</span> </span> MeSH-Augmented Recurrence within a Prelude-Recurrent-Coda Structure</figcaption>
<div id="alg2.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="alg2.l1" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">1:</span>
            
            
          
</div>
<div id="alg2.l2" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">2:</span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> Token embeddings <math id="alg2.l2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\text{emb}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>emb</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\text{emb}}</annotation></semantics></math>, Prelude <math id="alg2.l2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{pre}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>pre</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{pre}}</annotation></semantics></math>, Core <math id="alg2.l2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{core}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>core</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{core}}</annotation></semantics></math>, Coda <math id="alg2.l2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\text{coda}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>coda</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\text{coda}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l3" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">3:</span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Parameters:</span> MeSH buffer <math id="alg2.l3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝐌</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, Routers <math id="alg2.l3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{R_{\text{write}}^{(t)},R_{\text{read}}^{(t)}\}_{t=-1}^{K-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mtext>write</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>R</mi><mtext>read</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{R_{\text{write}}^{(t)},R_{\text{read}}^{(t)}\}_{t=-1}^{K-1}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l4" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">4:</span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Hyperparameters:</span> Loop iterations <math id="alg2.l4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math>, Buffer slots <math id="alg2.l4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l5" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">5:</span>
            
            
          
</div>
<div id="alg2.l6" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">6:</span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l6.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;"># 1. Initialize MeSH Buffer</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l7" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">7:</span>
            
            
          <math id="alg2.l7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}^{(0)}\leftarrow\text{zeros}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐌</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mtext>zeros</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}^{(0)}\leftarrow\text{zeros}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l7.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">{Initialize buffer with zeros}</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l8" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">8:</span>
            
            
          <math id="alg2.l8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}_{0}^{(0)}\leftarrow\mathbf{h}_{\text{emb}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐦</mi><mn>0</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>emb</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}_{0}^{(0)}\leftarrow\mathbf{h}_{\text{emb}}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l8.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">{Place embeddings in the first slot}</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l9" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">9:</span>
            
            
          
</div>
<div id="alg2.l10" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">10:</span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l10.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;"># 2. Prelude</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l11" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">11:</span>
            
            
          <math id="alg2.l11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(-1)}\leftarrow f_{\text{pre}}(\mathbf{h}_{\text{emb}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>pre</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>emb</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(-1)}\leftarrow f_{\text{pre}}(\mathbf{h}_{\text{emb}})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l11.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">{Compute prelude output}</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l12" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">12:</span>
            
            
          <math id="alg2.l12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{\text{write}}^{(-1)},\mathbf{w}_{\text{read}}^{(-1)}\leftarrow\text{Routers}^{(t=-1)}(\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(-1)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mtext>write</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mtext>read</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>Routers</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{\text{write}}^{(-1)},\mathbf{w}_{\text{read}}^{(-1)}\leftarrow\text{Routers}^{(t=-1)}(\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(-1)})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l12.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">{Use transitional routers}</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l13" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">13:</span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l13.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=0</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l13.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">to</span> <math id="alg2.l13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B-1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l13.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span> <span id="alg2.l13.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l14" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">14:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}_{b}^{(0)}\leftarrow\mathbf{m}_{b}^{(0)}+\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(-1)}\odot\mathbf{w}_{\text{write},b}^{(-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mtext>write</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}_{b}^{(0)}\leftarrow\mathbf{m}_{b}^{(0)}+\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(-1)}\odot\mathbf{w}_{\text{write},b}^{(-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l14.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">{Write prelude output to buffer}</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l15" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">15:</span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l15.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l15.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l16" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">16:</span>
            
            
          <math id="alg2.l16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{(0)}\leftarrow\sum_{b=0}^{B-1}\mathbf{m}_{b}^{(0)}\odot\mathbf{w}_{\text{read},b}^{(-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mtext>read</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{(0)}\leftarrow\sum_{b=0}^{B-1}\mathbf{m}_{b}^{(0)}\odot\mathbf{w}_{\text{read},b}^{(-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l16.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">{Synthesize first loop state}</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l17" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">17:</span>
            
            
          
</div>
<div id="alg2.l18" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">18:</span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l18.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;"># 3. Main Recursive Loop</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l19" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">19:</span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l19.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t=0</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l19.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">to</span> <math id="alg2.l19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K-1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l19.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span> <span id="alg2.l19.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l20" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">20:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}\leftarrow f_{\text{core}}(\mathbf{h}^{(t)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>core</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}\leftarrow f_{\text{core}}(\mathbf{h}^{(t)})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l20.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">{Core computation}</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l21" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">21:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{\text{write}}^{(t)},\mathbf{w}_{\text{read}}^{(t)}\leftarrow\text{Routers}^{(t)}(\mathbf{h}^{(t)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mtext>write</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mtext>read</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msup><mtext>Routers</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{\text{write}}^{(t)},\mathbf{w}_{\text{read}}^{(t)}\leftarrow\text{Routers}^{(t)}(\mathbf{h}^{(t)})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l21.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">{Compute step-wise weights}</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l22" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">22:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}^{(t+1)}\leftarrow\mathbf{M}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐌</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msup><mi>𝐌</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}^{(t+1)}\leftarrow\mathbf{M}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l23" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">23:</span>
            
            
            <span id="alg2.l23.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span> <math id="alg2.l23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b=0</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l23.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">to</span> <math id="alg2.l23.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B-1</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l23.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span> <span id="alg2.l23.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">do</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l24" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">24:</span>
            
            
             <math id="alg2.l24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}_{b}^{(t+1)}\leftarrow\mathbf{m}_{b}^{(t+1)}+\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}\odot\mathbf{w}_{\text{write},b}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mtext>write</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}_{b}^{(t+1)}\leftarrow\mathbf{m}_{b}^{(t+1)}+\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}\odot\mathbf{w}_{\text{write},b}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l24.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">{Update buffer with a distributed write}</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l25" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">25:</span>
            
            
            <span id="alg2.l25.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l25.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l26" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">26:</span>
            
            
            <math id="alg2.l26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{(t+1)}\leftarrow\sum_{b=0}^{B-1}\mathbf{m}_{b}^{(t+1)}\odot\mathbf{w}_{\text{read},b}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo rspace="0.111em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msubsup><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mtext>read</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{(t+1)}\leftarrow\sum_{b=0}^{B-1}\mathbf{m}_{b}^{(t+1)}\odot\mathbf{w}_{\text{read},b}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l26.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">{Synthesize next state}</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l27" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">27:</span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l27.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">end</span> <span id="alg2.l27.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">for</span>
</div>
<div id="alg2.l28" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">28:</span>
            
            
          
</div>
<div id="alg2.l29" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">29:</span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l29.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;"># 4. Final Coda Processing</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l30" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">30:</span>
            
            
          <math id="alg2.l30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\text{final}}\leftarrow f_{\text{coda}}(\mathbf{h}^{(K)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>final</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>coda</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>K</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\text{final}}\leftarrow f_{\text{coda}}(\mathbf{h}^{(K)})</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="alg2.l30.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#808080;">{Use the state after the last read}</span>

</div>
<div id="alg2.l31" class="ltx_listingline">
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_listingline">31:</span>
            
            
          <span id="alg2.l31.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="alg2.l31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\text{final}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>final</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\text{final}}</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Discussion: Expressive Power of MeSH as a General Recurrence</h2>

<div id="A4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The baseline recurrences described in Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Preliminaries: Architecture of Recursive Transformers ‣ 3 Methodology: Alleviating Information Overload and Enabling Functional Specialization ‣ MeSH: Memory-as-State-Highways for Recursive Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> employ a fixed, non-adaptive state update rule: the output of the core block, <math id="A4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}=f_{\text{core}}(\mathbf{h}^{(t)})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mtext>core</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}=f_{\text{core}}(\mathbf{h}^{(t)})</annotation></semantics></math>, is always supplemented by a predetermined state (e.g., zero, the previous state <math id="A4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, or the initial state <math id="A4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math>). We propose that MeSH offers a more general and powerful alternative by replacing this rigid addition with a learnable, dynamic state composition mechanism.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Proposition 2.1.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">The MeSH recurrence, defined by the compute-write-read cycle, generalizes the concept of additive state updates (as in residual and anchor variants) by learning to dynamically retrieve and combine historical states from memory to form the state for the next iteration.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Demonstration.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To reveal the underlying mechanics, we can unroll the MeSH update equations. The state for the next iteration, <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{(t+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{(t+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>, is formed by reading from the just-updated memory <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{M}^{(t+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐌</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{M}^{(t+1)}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="A4.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A4.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbf{h}^{(t+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbf{h}^{(t+1)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{b=0}^{B-1}\mathbf{m}_{b}^{(t+1)}\odot\mathbf{w}_{\text{read},b}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mtext>read</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{b=0}^{B-1}\mathbf{m}_{b}^{(t+1)}\odot\mathbf{w}_{\text{read},b}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sum_{b=0}^{B-1}\left(\mathbf{m}_{b}^{(t)}+\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}\odot\mathbf{w}_{\text{write},b}^{(t)}\right)\odot\mathbf{w}_{\text{read},b}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mtext>write</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mtext>read</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sum_{b=0}^{B-1}\left(\mathbf{m}_{b}^{(t)}+\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}\odot\mathbf{w}_{\text{write},b}^{(t)}\right)\odot\mathbf{w}_{\text{read},b}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A4.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\underbrace{\sum_{b=0}^{B-1}\mathbf{m}_{b}^{(t)}\odot\mathbf{w}_{\text{read},b}^{(t)}}_{\text{Retrieved Historical Summary}}+\underbrace{\left(\sum_{b=0}^{B-1}\mathbf{w}_{\text{write},b}^{(t)}\odot\mathbf{w}_{\text{read},b}^{(t)}\right)}_{\text{Gating Factor}}\odot\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mtext>read</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Retrieved Historical Summary</mtext></munder><mo>+</mo><mrow><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover></mstyle><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mtext>write</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mrow><mtext>read</mtext><mo>,</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mtext>Gating Factor</mtext></munder><mo rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\underbrace{\sum_{b=0}^{B-1}\mathbf{m}_{b}^{(t)}\odot\mathbf{w}_{\text{read},b}^{(t)}}_{\text{Retrieved Historical Summary}}+\underbrace{\left(\sum_{b=0}^{B-1}\mathbf{w}_{\text{write},b}^{(t)}\odot\mathbf{w}_{\text{read},b}^{(t)}\right)}_{\text{Gating Factor}}\odot\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Let us analyze the two resulting components. The second term is the core’s output, <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, scaled by a learned gating factor. The first term is a dynamic retrieval of information from the memory state <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐦</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">before</span> the current write operation. Note that this term is distinct from the previous state <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, which was formed using the read weights from the prior step, <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{w}_{\text{read}}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐰</mi><mtext>read</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{w}_{\text{read}}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The formulation reveals that the next state <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{(t+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{(t+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is a generalized residual update composed of:</p>
<ol id="A4.I1" class="ltx_enumerate" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:20pt;">
<li id="A4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="A4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">retrieved historical summary</span> that dynamically combines states presented in the buffer. The read router learns what historical information is most relevant at this step.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="A4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A <span id="A4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">gated output</span> of the current core block, <math id="A4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, scaled by a learned gating factor.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The dynamic process generalizes the fixed baseline recurrences, as we can conceptually unroll the <span id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">retrieved historical summary</span> even further as a weighted combination of the initial memory state and all previous core outputs <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(0)},\dots,\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t-1)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(0)},\dots,\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t-1)}\}</annotation></semantics></math> that have been written to the buffer. Therefore, the next state <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{(t+1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{(t+1)}</annotation></semantics></math> can be viewed as a comprehensive, dynamic aggregation of all computations performed so far:</p>
<table id="A4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="A4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{(t+1)}=\alpha_{t}\odot\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}+\sum_{i=0}^{t-1}\alpha_{i}\odot\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(i)}+\alpha_{\text{emb}}\odot\mathbf{h}_{\text{emb}}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></munderover><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mtext>emb</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⊙</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>emb</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{(t+1)}=\alpha_{t}\odot\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}+\sum_{i=0}^{t-1}\alpha_{i}\odot\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(i)}+\alpha_{\text{emb}}\odot\mathbf{h}_{\text{emb}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where all coefficients <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> are implicit coupled with write and read weights during previous iterations. The perspective makes the generalization self-evident:</p>
<ul id="A4.I2" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simulating anchor (<math id="A4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}+\mathbf{h}_{\text{emb}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">m</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">emb</mtext></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}+\mathbf{h}_{\text{emb}}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span>
This is achieved by learning a routing scheme where the coefficients in Eq. <a href="#A4.E7" title="In Demonstration. ‣ Appendix D Discussion: Expressive Power of MeSH as a General Recurrence ‣ MeSH: Memory-as-State-Highways for Recursive Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> are set as follows: the weight for the current computation, <math id="A4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>, approaches 1; the weight for the initial embedding, <math id="A4.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\text{emb}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mtext>emb</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\text{emb}}</annotation></semantics></math>, approaches 1; and all other historical weights, <math id="A4.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i&lt;t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i&lt;t}</annotation></semantics></math>, are driven to zero. MeSH can learn to adopt this specific weighting only when needed, rather than being hard-wired to it.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Simulating residual (<math id="A4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}+\mathbf{h}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">m</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}+\mathbf{h}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>):</span>
To approximate this, MeSH needs to reconstruct the previous state, <math id="A4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, as its historical summary. This is naturally achievable. Since <math id="A4.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> is itself a weighted sum of <math id="A4.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{h}_{\text{emb}},\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(0)},\dots,\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t-1)}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>emb</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{h}_{\text{emb}},\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(0)},\dots,\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t-1)}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the routers at step <math id="A4.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> can learn to compute the appropriate coefficients (<math id="A4.I2.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{\text{emb}},\alpha_{0},\dots,\alpha_{t-1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mtext>emb</mtext></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{\text{emb}},\alpha_{0},\dots,\alpha_{t-1}</annotation></semantics></math>) to reconstruct or closely approximate <math id="A4.I2.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>. More powerfully, MeSH can choose to form a “better” historical summary by up-weighting more relevant past states (e.g., <math id="A4.I2.i2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t-5)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t-5)}</annotation></semantics></math>) and down-weighting irrelevant ones (e.g., <math id="A4.I2.i2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>), thus forming more effective long-range dependencies.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A4.I2.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Adaptive Combination:</span> The core advantage is that the coefficients <math id="A4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> are not fixed. They are functions of the current state <math id="A4.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>, allowing the model to change its recurrence rule on the fly. It can learn to behave like an Anchor in early steps, transition to a Residual-like update, and synthesize a complex summary from multiple past states for the final output, all within a single forward pass.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In conclusion, MeSH does not just replicate the fixed recurrences; it subsumes the underlying principle of combining past and present information into a flexible, learnable framework. It replaces the hard-coded “what to add” (e.g., <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{(0)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{(0)}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>) with a learned “what to retrieve and combine,” offering a substantially more expressive mechanism for managing state in recursive transformers.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>More Results</h2>

<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Ablation Study: MeSH Buffer Length</h3>

<figure id="A5.T3" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Ablation on MeSH buffer length for the Pythia-410M model (<math id="A5.T3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{loop}}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>loop</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{loop}}=2</annotation></semantics></math>). Performance is optimized with <math id="A5.T3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math> auxiliary “scratchpad” slots.</figcaption>
<table id="A5.T3.2" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A5.T3.2.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A5.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scratchpad Slots (<math id="A5.T3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></td>
<td id="A5.T3.2.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A5.T3.2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Buffer Length (<math id="A5.T3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=(N_{\text{loop}}+1)+k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_bold">loop</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=(N_{\text{loop}}+1)+k</annotation></semantics></math>)</span></td>
<td id="A5.T3.2.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A5.T3.2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Loss <math id="A5.T3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
<td id="A5.T3.2.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A5.T3.2.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PPL <math id="A5.T3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></span></td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T3.2.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T3.2.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">0</td>
<td id="A5.T3.2.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t"><math id="A5.T3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2+1)+0=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2+1)+0=3</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A5.T3.2.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">2.2108</td>
<td id="A5.T3.2.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">9.1231</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T3.2.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T3.2.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">1</td>
<td id="A5.T3.2.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><math id="A5.T3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2+1)+1=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2+1)+1=4</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A5.T3.2.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">2.2083</td>
<td id="A5.T3.2.3.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">9.1003</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T3.2.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T3.2.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;"><span id="A5.T3.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;">2</span></td>
<td id="A5.T3.2.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;"><math id="A5.T3.m11" class="ltx_Math" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;" alttext="(2+1)+2=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;" mathbackground="#D5E8D4" stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;" mathbackground="#D5E8D4">2</mn><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;" mathbackground="#D5E8D4">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;" mathbackground="#D5E8D4">1</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;" mathbackground="#D5E8D4" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;" mathbackground="#D5E8D4">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;" mathbackground="#D5E8D4">2</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;" mathbackground="#D5E8D4">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;" mathbackground="#D5E8D4">5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2+1)+2=5</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A5.T3.2.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;"><span id="A5.T3.2.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;">2.2077</span></td>
<td id="A5.T3.2.4.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;"><span id="A5.T3.2.4.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;">9.0944</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T3.2.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T3.2.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">3</td>
<td id="A5.T3.2.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><math id="A5.T3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2+1)+3=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2+1)+3=6</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A5.T3.2.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">2.2092</td>
<td id="A5.T3.2.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">9.1088</td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To establish a principled heuristic for setting the MeSH buffer length (<math id="A5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>), we hypothesize that its capacity should scale with the number of major computational states generated during the recursive process. For a model with <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{loop}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>loop</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{loop}}</annotation></semantics></math> iterations, this includes the initial state from the prelude network plus the output from each of the <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{loop}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>loop</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{loop}}</annotation></semantics></math> core blocks, totaling <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{loop}}+1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>loop</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{loop}}+1</annotation></semantics></math> essential states. We therefore model the buffer size as <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=(N_{\text{loop}}+1)+k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>loop</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=(N_{\text{loop}}+1)+k</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="A5.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of auxiliary “scratchpad” slots available for flexible composition.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct an ablation study to find the optimal <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> using the Pythia-410M model with a <span id="A5.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">4+8R2+4</span> configuration, where <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{\text{loop}}=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>loop</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{\text{loop}}=2</annotation></semantics></math>. The results are shown in Table <a href="#A5.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ E.1 Ablation Study: MeSH Buffer Length ‣ Appendix E More Results ‣ MeSH: Memory-as-State-Highways for Recursive Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. Performance improves as we add scratchpad slots, peaking at <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=2</annotation></semantics></math>. This configuration, corresponding to a total buffer length of <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=5</annotation></semantics></math>, achieves the lowest validation loss and perplexity. Performance slightly degrades at <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=3</annotation></semantics></math>, suggesting a point of diminishing returns. This indicates a sweet spot where the buffer has dedicated slots for each major computational state, plus two auxiliary slots for managing intermediate representations, without making the routing task overly complex. Based on empirical results, we adopt the general rule <math id="A5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B=N_{\text{loop}}+3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mtext>loop</mtext></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B=N_{\text{loop}}+3</annotation></semantics></math> for all MeSH models in our main experiments.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Ablation Study: Heuristic State-Passing Schemes</h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While individual heuristic schemes like <span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">+anchor</span> and <span id="A5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">+residual</span> improve over the base recursive model, a natural question arises: can we achieve further gains by combining them, and can the model learn the optimal combination? To investigate this, we conducted an ablation study on the Pythia-410M model (4+8R2+4 configuration) exploring both fixed additive combinations and learnable linear combinations of supplementary states (<math id="A5.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}_{\text{sup}}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>sup</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}_{\text{sup}}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>).
For the learnable schemes, we define the supplementary state as a combination of the base, anchor, anchor*, and residual states:
<math id="A5.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{(t)}=\alpha_{1}\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}+\alpha_{2}\mathbf{h}^{(0)}+\alpha_{3}\mathbf{h}_{\text{emb}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>m</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝐡</mi><mtext>emb</mtext></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{(t)}=\alpha_{1}\mathbf{h}_{\text{m}}^{(t)}+\alpha_{2}\mathbf{h}^{(0)}+\alpha_{3}\mathbf{h}_{\text{emb}}</annotation></semantics></math>. We test two variants for the coefficients <math id="A5.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>α</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ul id="A5.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="A5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Static Combination:</span> The coefficients are trainable scalar parameters that are fixed after training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ng &amp; Wang, 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Dynamic Combination:</span> The coefficients are dynamically computed at each iteration based on the previous state <math id="A5.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{h}^{(t-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>𝐡</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{h}^{(t-1)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results, summarized in Table <a href="#A5.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ E.2 Ablation Study: Heuristic State-Passing Schemes ‣ Appendix E More Results ‣ MeSH: Memory-as-State-Highways for Recursive Transformers" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, reveal the brittleness of heuristic design. Simply adding all states (<span id="A5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">+residual+anchor+anchor*</span>) degrades performance, yielding a higher perplexity (9.24) than using <span id="A5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">+anchor</span> alone (9.19). This confirms that a naive “more is better” approach is not a reliable strategy. While a carefully hand-picked combination (<span id="A5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">+anchor+anchor*</span>) achieves the best result among all explicit schemes (9.17 PPL), this requires manual tuning. The learnable <span id="A5.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">static</span> and <span id="A5.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">dynamic</span> combinations effectively avoid the worst-case performance degradation but fail to match the best-performing heuristic.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A5.T4" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 4: </span>Ablation on combinations of supplementary context schemes for Pythia-410M (4+8R2+4). We report evaluation loss and perplexity on the Pile dataset. While a hand-picked combination (<span id="A5.T4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">+anchor+anchor*</span>) works best among heuristics, MeSH surpasses all explicit schemes.</figcaption>
<table id="A5.T4.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A5.T4.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T4.4.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_tt"><span id="A5.T4.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Scheme</span></td>
<td id="A5.T4.4.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A5.T4.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Loss</span> <math id="A5.T4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td id="A5.T4.4.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt"><span id="A5.T4.4.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">PPL</span> <math id="A5.T4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T4.4.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T4.4.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;"><span id="A5.T4.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">vanilla</span></td>
<td id="A5.T4.4.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;"><span id="A5.T4.4.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">2.2047</span></td>
<td id="A5.T4.4.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;"><span id="A5.T4.4.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#E6E6E6;">9.0675</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T4.4.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T4.4.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFEBBE;"><span id="A5.T4.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFEBBE;">recursive-base</span></td>
<td id="A5.T4.4.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFEBBE;"><span id="A5.T4.4.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFEBBE;">2.2358</span></td>
<td id="A5.T4.4.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFEBBE;"><span id="A5.T4.4.3.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFEBBE;">9.3542</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T4.4.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T4.4.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" colspan="3"><span id="A5.T4.4.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Single Heuristic Baselines</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T4.4.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T4.4.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">+anchor</td>
<td id="A5.T4.4.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">2.2178</td>
<td id="A5.T4.4.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">9.1867</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T4.4.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T4.4.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" colspan="3"><span id="A5.T4.4.6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fixed Additive Combinations</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T4.4.7" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T4.4.7.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">+residual+anchor</td>
<td id="A5.T4.4.7.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">2.2251</td>
<td id="A5.T4.4.7.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">9.2541</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T4.4.8" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T4.4.8.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">+residual+anchor+anchor*</td>
<td id="A5.T4.4.8.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">2.2237</td>
<td id="A5.T4.4.8.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">9.2415</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T4.4.9" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T4.4.9.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">+anchor+anchor*</td>
<td id="A5.T4.4.9.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A5.T4.4.9.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">2.2159</span></td>
<td id="A5.T4.4.9.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center"><span id="A5.T4.4.9.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">9.1694</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T4.4.10" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T4.4.10.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" colspan="3"><span id="A5.T4.4.10.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Learnable Combinations</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T4.4.11" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T4.4.11.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t">+static comb.</td>
<td id="A5.T4.4.11.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">2.2163</td>
<td id="A5.T4.4.11.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t">9.1731</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T4.4.12" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T4.4.12.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left">+dynamic comb.</td>
<td id="A5.T4.4.12.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">2.2176</td>
<td id="A5.T4.4.12.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center">9.1851</td></tr>
<tr id="A5.T4.4.13" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A5.T4.4.13.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;"><span id="A5.T4.4.13.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;">+mesh (ours)</span></td>
<td id="A5.T4.4.13.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;"><span id="A5.T4.4.13.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;">2.2077</span></td>
<td id="A5.T4.4.13.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;"><span id="A5.T4.4.13.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5E8D4;">9.0944</span></td></tr>
</table>
</figure>
<div id="A5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">This suggests that while explicit, learnable weighting can provide a “safe” baseline by ignoring detrimental combinations, it lacks the expressive capacity to discover optimal synergistic interactions. In sharp contrast, our <span id="A5.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">+mesh</span> model (9.09 PPL) significantly outperforms all heuristic-based approaches. Instead of being constrained to an explicit, low-dimensional linear combination of predefined states, MeSH learns a complex, non-linear function for retrieving and composing information from its memory buffer. This allows it to discover implicit, high-dimensional combinations, effectively breaking through the performance ceiling imposed by simpler, explicit state-passing schemes.</p>
</div>
</section>
